//? Title: Bilinear Interpolation: Four Corners, Two Linear Blends
//? Subtitle: Hand-checked weights, exactness, extrapolation and a second-order error bound
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Given values on a rectangular grid, estimate the value between grid nodes
without fitting a global surface. Four nearby samples determine a continuous
interpolant that is linear in each coordinate separately. We derive its weights,
check one uneven cell by hand, reconstruct the original sine–cosine example,
and verify an exact quadratic error formula. The datamunge API also extrapolates
outside the grid, where its in-cell range and error guarantees no longer apply.
)

\toc

> The method

For a cell \([x_i,x_{i+1}]\times[y_j,y_{j+1}]\), define fractional coordinates

$$
t=\frac{x-x_i}{x_{i+1}-x_i},\qquad u=\frac{y-y_j}{y_{j+1}-y_j}.
$$
\alttext(t and u are the query's relative positions along the cell's x and y directions.)

Interpolate along the lower and upper rows, then between them:

$$
a=(1-t)z_{00}+tz_{10},\quad b=(1-t)z_{01}+tz_{11},\quad
I(x,y)=(1-u)a+ub=\sum_{r,s\in\{0,1\}}w_{rs}z_{rs},
$$
\alttext(Blend the two lower-row corners, blend the two upper-row corners, then blend those results in y.)

The weights, in corner order \(00,10,01,11\), are
\(((1-t)(1-u),t(1-u),(1-t)u,tu)\). Their sum is one. Inside the
cell they are nonnegative, so interpolation cannot leave the range of the four
corner values. The rule reproduces \(1,x,y,xy\) separately, hence every
\(a+bx+cy+dxy\). Reversing the order of the two linear blends gives the same
formula, apart from floating-point rounding.

Adjacent cells agree along their shared edge because both reduce to the same
one-dimensional interpolation there. Values are continuous, but derivatives can
jump across grid lines. Uneven spacing is allowed; the grid need only be
rectangular with strictly increasing axes.

> Simple example: an uneven cell

Take \(x\in[0,2]\), \(y\in[0,4]\), corners
\((z_{00},z_{10},z_{01},z_{11})=(0,2,4,10)\), and query \((0.5,3)\).
Then \(t=1/4\), \(u=3/4\), and the four weights are
\((3,1,9,3)/16\). The lower and upper row blends are \(a=0.5\) and \(b=5.5\),
so the final result is \(0.25(0.5)+0.75(5.5)=4.25\).

```{cpp, file="figures/bilinear_cell.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  dm::graphics::Diagram diagram(100,800);
  diagram.path({{0,0},{2,0},{2,4},{0,4},{0,0}})
         .path({{0,3},{2,3}}).path({{.5,0},{.5,4}})
         .ellipse({.5,3},.07,.07,{220,38,38})
         .text({1,4.6}, "(0,4): z = 4            (2,4): z = 10")
         .text({1,-.6}, "(0,0): z = 0            (2,0): z = 2")
         .text({3.8,3}, "Query (0.5,3)\nt = 1/4, u = 3/4\nI = 4.25");
  diagram.save_svg("build/bilinear_cell.svg");
}
```
// result_begin:
// \image(figures/bilinear_cell.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The unequal physical cell widths and heights share one coordinate scale. Crossing lines locate the query, three quarters of the way up and one quarter across.)
\alttext(A two-by-four rectangular cell has corner values zero, two, four and ten. A red dot at x one half and y three marks the interpolation query.)

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::print("| query x | query y | interpolated value |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for (const auto& [x,y] : std::vector<std::pair<double,double>>{{.5,3},{0,0},{2,4},{1,0},{3,3}})
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} |\n", x,y,
        dm::algorithms::bilinear_interpolate({0,2},{0,4},{0,4,2,10},x,y));
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| query x | query y | interpolated value |
| ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 0.5 | 3 | 4.25 |
| 0 | 0 | 0 |
| 2 | 4 | 10 |
| 1 | 0 | 1 |
| 3 | 3 | 10.5 |
// result_end
\caption(The samples use x-major storage: `{0,4,2,10}`. At x=3 the library extrapolates; the value can exceed every corner.)

The stored sequence is \(z_{00},z_{01},z_{10},z_{11}\), since
`z[i*ys.size()+j]` varies y fastest. This differs from the order used when
listing the four weights above. At the outside query \((3,3)\), \(t=1.5\),
so two weights are negative and the result is 10.5. To clamp a query instead,
explicitly clamp its coordinates before calling the API.

> Checking exactness and the original smooth reconstruction

The original report sampled \(f(x,y)=\sin x\cos y\) on a \(6\times6\) grid
over \([0,\pi]^2\), then evaluated the diagonal \(x=y=s\). The complete
program below retains that experiment. It also checks the original bilinear
polynomial \(1-2x+0.5y+0.3xy\) at \((1.7,2.3)\), whose exact value is
\(-0.077\). The polynomial check is a numerical exactness check; the sinusoid
has curvature that a bilinear patch cannot reproduce.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/bilinear_diagonal.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const double pi=std::acos(-1.);
  std::vector<double> axis,z,polynomial;
  for (int i=0;i<6;++i) axis.push_back(pi*i/5);
  for (double x:axis) for (double y:axis) {
    z.push_back(std::sin(x)*std::cos(y));
    polynomial.push_back(1-2*x+.5*y+.3*x*y);
  }
  std::vector<double> s,truth,interpolated;
  double maximum=0;
  for (int i=0;i<=300;++i) {
    const double x=pi*i/300;
    s.push_back(x);
    truth.push_back(std::sin(x)*std::cos(x));
    interpolated.push_back(dm::algorithms::bilinear_interpolate(axis,axis,z,x,x));
    maximum=std::max(maximum,std::abs(interpolated.back()-truth.back()));
  }
  std::print("| check | value |\n| :--- | ---: |\n| polynomial at (1.7,2.3) | {:.12f} |\n"
             "| largest sampled diagonal error | {:.9f} |\n| whole-cell smooth bound h squared / 4 | {:.9f} |\n",
             dm::algorithms::bilinear_interpolate(axis,axis,polynomial,1.7,2.3), maximum,pi*pi/100);
  dm::graphics::RPlot::create()
      .lines(s,truth,"exact",dm::RGB{16,150,100},2)
      .lines(s,interpolated,"bilinear",dm::RGB{220,38,38},2)
      .title("Sine–cosine diagonal from a 6 by 6 grid")
      .x_label("s, where x = y = s").y_label("value").size(800,440)
      .save_svg("build/bilinear_diagonal.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| check | value |
| :--- | ---: |
| polynomial at (1.7,2.3) | -0.077000000000 |
| largest sampled diagonal error | 0.045609230 |
| whole-cell smooth bound h squared / 4 | 0.098696044 |
\image(figures/bilinear_diagonal.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The diagonal samples are compared with the exact sinusoid. The reported sampled maximum is not a certified continuous maximum.)
\alttext(The bilinear reconstruction follows the sine–cosine diagonal, with visible differences near its positive and negative peaks.)

> Deriving and checking the error rate

Write the two one-dimensional interpolation operators as \(I_x,I_y\).
The identity \(f-I_xI_yf=(f-I_xf)+I_x(f-I_yf)\), the one-dimensional
linear interpolation remainder, and nonnegative interpolation weights give

$$
|f-I_xI_yf|\le\frac{h_x^2}{8}\sup|f_{xx}|+
                     \frac{h_y^2}{8}\sup|f_{yy}|.
$$
\alttext(The in-cell absolute error is bounded by one eighth of each squared cell width times the largest second derivative in that direction.)

Thus smooth functions have second-order error as the cell widths decrease.
For the sinusoid, both second derivatives are bounded in magnitude by 1, so
equal widths \(h\) give the bound \(h^2/4\) printed above.
For \(q(x,y)=x^2+y^2\), the identity is sharper and exact:

$$
I q-q=(x-x_i)(x_{i+1}-x)+(y-y_j)(y_{j+1}-y),
\qquad (I q-q)_{\mathrm{midpoint}}=\frac{h_x^2+h_y^2}{4}.
$$
\alttext(The quadratic interpolation error is the sum of two products of distances to the cell edges. At a cell midpoint it equals one quarter of the sum of squared cell widths.)

On a uniform unit-square grid with \(m\) intervals in each direction, every
cell midpoint has error \(1/(2m^2)\). Doubling m must divide the error by four.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/bilinear_error.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  std::vector<double> resolution,measured,predicted;
  std::print("| intervals per axis | largest midpoint error | exact error | ratio to previous |\n"
             "| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  double previous=0;
  for (int m:{1,2,4,8,16,32}) {
    std::vector<double> axis,z;
    for (int i=0;i<=m;++i) axis.push_back(double(i)/m);
    for (double x:axis) for (double y:axis) z.push_back(x*x+y*y);
    double error=0;
    for (int i=0;i<m;++i) for (int j=0;j<m;++j) {
      const double x=(i+.5)/m,y=(j+.5)/m;
      error=std::max(error,std::abs(dm::algorithms::bilinear_interpolate(axis,axis,z,x,y)-x*x-y*y));
    }
    resolution.push_back(m); measured.push_back(error); predicted.push_back(.5/(m*m));
    std::print("| {} | {:.10f} | {:.10f} | {} |\n",m,error,predicted.back(),previous?previous/error:0);
    previous=error;
  }
  dm::graphics::RPlot::create()
      .lines(resolution,predicted,"exact 1 / (2m squared)",dm::RGB{148,163,184},3)
      .lines(resolution,measured,"measured",dm::RGB{37,99,235},1.5)
      .title("Quadratic midpoint error")
      .x_label("intervals per axis").y_label("absolute error").size(800,440)
      .save_svg("build/bilinear_error.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| intervals per axis | largest midpoint error | exact error | ratio to previous |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 1 | 0.5000000000 | 0.5000000000 | 0 |
| 2 | 0.1250000000 | 0.1250000000 | 4 |
| 4 | 0.0312500000 | 0.0312500000 | 4 |
| 8 | 0.0078125000 | 0.0078125000 | 4 |
| 16 | 0.0019531250 | 0.0019531250 | 4 |
| 32 | 0.0004882812 | 0.0004882812 | 4 |
\image(figures/bilinear_error.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The exact formula and observed errors coincide. The first ratio is zero because there is no previous grid; every later ratio is four.)
\alttext(Quadratic interpolation error decreases from one half on a single cell to about 0.000488 on a grid with 32 intervals per axis.)

> API and numerical limits

`bilinear_interpolate` rejects axes with fewer than two points, repeated or
decreasing coordinates, nonfinite data or queries, and a sample count other than
`xs.size()*ys.size()`. It checks the full grid on each call, costing
\(O(n_xn_y+n_x+n_y)\) validation time and constant auxiliary storage.
After validation, binary cell lookup costs \(O(\log n_x+\log n_y)\) and
the two blends use four samples. This distinguishes the interpolation kernel's
constant work from the checked API's preprocessing cost.

The implementation uses stable linear blends so a finite in-cell result can
survive corner differences that would overflow ordinary subtraction. An
unrepresentable coordinate or result raises an overflow error. Near-duplicate
coordinates can still amplify data and coordinate noise; rescale poorly
conditioned axes. Extrapolation uses the nearest end cell and can overshoot.
Neither the convex-hull statement nor the interior smoothness bound applies
outside the sampled rectangle.

> Conclusion

The uneven-cell calculation gives 4.25 and confirms the sample layout. The
original smooth reconstruction retains its approximation error, while the
quadratic control exactly exhibits a factor-of-four improvement on each grid
refinement. These generated controls make a separate observed dataset unnecessary.
Bilinear interpolation provides continuity and a second-order smooth-data error
bound, with derivative jumps and potentially unstable extrapolation as limits.

> References

- Weiser, A. and Zarantonello, S. E. (1988). *A note on piecewise linear and multilinear table interpolation in many dimensions*. Mathematics of Computation, 50(181), 189–196. https://doi.org/10.1090/S0025-5718-1988-0917826-0
- SciPy. *RegularGridInterpolator*. The comparable configuration is linear interpolation with bounds_error=False and fill_value=None; datamunge accepts only finite values and increasing axes. https://docs.scipy.org/doc/scipy/reference/generated/scipy.interpolate.RegularGridInterpolator.html
